                                  THE PLAN



  “This is really boring,” said Manny.
  “That’s the point.”
  “I’m supposed to be good at rhythm.”
  “You’re good at rhythm. You’re terrible at waiting.”
  He tapped twice and, with great gravity, a third time, to see what I’d
do.
  I tapped back three.
  He laughed so hard he had to hold his ribs.



At the end of the night I wrote the plan on a single sheet. Keep the
doors open. Break the Moment. Get people out. Let the evidence travel
to where it can be read. Beneath that I wrote Mikey, crossed it out, and
wrote anyone who asks to leave. He was too important to make the single
person in the room.
   Mamá checked my best shirt for buttons. She’d taken the fourteenth
off work, and when I asked why, she gave me the look.
   “Somebody has to cook,” she said.
   I came close to telling her she didn’t have to be brave. She had a
damp shirt in her hand, and I valued my face. So I told her where Yari
would be, where I’d walk in, who held the phone numbers if mine
died. She copied them onto a slip of paper, though they were already in
her phone, and filed it in the drawer next to the yellow school form.
   Saturday morning, a message from Vanessa.

   Vanessa: they changed the seating overnight

On paper the exit aisles were still open. The people from the old pilot
had been shifted inward behind the decorative screens. Mikey’s chair
now sat next to Vale’s.
  Mine was in the center.




